                "fields": [],
                "itemId": 17592186576278,
                "kind": "tutorial",
                "knowledgePoint": null,
                "markdown": "Recall that a matrix is square if the number of rows equals the number of columns. There are other types of square matrices with special names.\n\n- An **upper triangular** matrix is a square matrix in which all entries *below* the diagonal are zeros. For example, the following matrices are all upper triangular matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNote that we can have any number (including $0$) on the diagonal and above.\n\n- A **lower triangular** matrix is a square matrix in which all entries *above* the diagonal are zeros. For example, the following matrices are all lower triangular matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nAgain, note that we can have any number (including $0$) on the diagonal and below.",
                "mathAcademyId": "2983",
                "requiresCalculator": null,
                "solution": "",
                "status": "completed",
                "stepId": "17592186120618",
                "title": "Introduction"
              },
              "total": 4
            },
            {
              "number": 2,
              "order": 2,
              "step": {
                "canContinue": true,
                "contentId": 17592186119585,
                "elapsedSeconds": 47.448,
                "fields": [],
                "itemId": 17592186576279,
                "kind": "example",
                "knowledgePoint": {
                  "id": 17592186119771,
                  "title": "Describing a Triangular Matrix"
                },
                "markdown": "How can we best describe the following matrix? \n\n![](images/fc/fc2ed708d717844ca73b7ecbfd8467923fad091ae524153350e7e2fb3df1ea4e.png)",
                "mathAcademyId": "e-4394",
                "requiresCalculator": null,
                "solution": "Notice that $A$ is a matrix for which all entries above the diagonal are $0$'s:\n![](images/52/5260137f2dbf20b44400659be8467da333ff228e5bff323465f920a9505ee5f2.png)\n\nTherefore, it is a lower triangular matrix.\n\nIt is not an upper triangular matrix or a diagonal matrix since there is a non-zero element below the diagonal: $a_{21}=6 \\neq 0.$",
                "status": "completed",
                "stepId": "17592186120619",
                "title": "Describing a Triangular Matrix"
              },
              "total": 4
            }
